::PC_U05_L02_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U05_L02_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.2
Question:
Find the midpoint between 2 + 4i and 6 + 8i.
{
~A. 8 + 12i#This adds coordinates instead of averaging them. Midpoint requires averaging.
=B. 4 + 6i#Correct. Average real parts: (2 + 6)/2 = 4. Average imaginary parts: (4 + 8)/2 = 6.
~C. 2 + 2i#This subtracts and halves instead of averaging the original coordinates.
~D. 6 + 4i#This reverses the midpoint coordinates.
}
::PC_U05_L02_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U05_L02_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.2
Question:
Find the distance between 1 + 2i and 4 + 6i.
{
~A. 3#This uses only the real-coordinate difference. Distance must use both coordinates.
~B. 4#This uses only the imaginary-coordinate difference. Distance must use both coordinates.
~C. 7#This adds the coordinate differences instead of using the distance formula.
=D. 5#Correct. The coordinate differences are 3 and 4, so the distance is sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5.
}
::PC_U05_L02_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U05_L02_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.3
Question:
A complex number moves from -1 + i to 2 + 5i. Which coordinate change describes the movement?
{
=A. Right 3 and up 4#Correct. The real part changes from -1 to 2, which is right 3. The imaginary part changes from 1 to 5, which is up 4.
~B. Left 3 and up 4#This reverses the horizontal direction. The real coordinate increases.
~C. Right 4 and up 3#This switches the real and imaginary changes.
~D. Right 3 and down 4#This reverses the vertical direction. The imaginary coordinate increases.
}
::PC_U05_L02_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U05_L02_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.2
Question:
Which point represents the complex number -4 - 3i?
{
~A. (4, 3)#This changes both signs. Use the signs in the complex number.
~B. (-3, -4)#This reverses the real and imaginary coordinates.
=C. (-4, -3)#Correct. a + bi is represented by (a, b), so -4 - 3i is (-4, -3).
~D. (3, 4)#This changes signs and reverses the coordinates.
}
::PC_U05_L02_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U05_L02_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.3
Question:
Why can distance formula be used with complex numbers on the complex plane?
{
~A. Because imaginary numbers are not coordinates#This misunderstands the complex plane. The imaginary coefficient becomes the vertical coordinate.
=B. Because a + bi corresponds to the coordinate point (a, b)#Correct. Once a + bi is viewed as (a, b), coordinate geometry formulas apply.
~C. Because distance only uses real numbers#The final distance may be real, but both complex coordinates are used.
~D. Because midpoint and distance are the same calculation#Midpoint and distance are different calculations.
}